The '''Zong blockade of Daras''' was a naval blockade imposed by the [[Kingdom of Zong]] against the [[Socialist Republic of Daras]] from 1907 to 1911. Zong denied transit to vessels flying the Darasian flag in the [[Jade Sea]] and through the Jade Peninsula, making the blockade a preemptive show of force as well as an economic measure. The blockade was intended to constrain the new socialist state, discourage the spread of revolutionary politics in Fosia, and preserve Zong's strategic position in eastern maritime affairs.

The blockade formed part of the wider post-revolutionary settlement in eastern Fosia. After the [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] established Daras as the first socialist state in the region, Zong leaders viewed the new government as both an ideological threat and a potential rival in naval power. The Zong court also regarded the blockade as a continuation of its broader effort to secure maritime dominance after earlier conflicts had strengthened its position in [[Lurandia]] and the wider [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] sphere.

== Background ==

Following the revolution, Daras sought to preserve external trade while consolidating the new socialist state. Zong, by contrast, aimed to demonstrate that access to the Jade Sea remained subject to its approval. The blockade was therefore designed not only to pressure Daras economically, but also to signal to other Fosian states that socialist expansion would be met with maritime coercion.

The blockade had immediate commercial effects. Darasian shipping was forced away from the Jade Sea and toward the [[South Sea]], where winter freezing made naval trade unreliable for part of the year. Daras retained overland trade connections with [[Illypnia]], where it was still viewed as a more acceptable trading partner than Zong, in part because of the aftermath of the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] and Zong's dominance in Lurandia after the capture of Sant Cristòfor. The blockade also encouraged closer Darasian ties with [[Breisland]], [[Mercia]], and [[Volisania]], whose governments saw Daras as a useful counterweight to Zong.

== Blockade ==

Zong enforced the blockade by denying all ships carrying the Darasian flag the right to transit the Jade Sea. The policy effectively restricted Daras' access to the primary eastern maritime corridor and forced the socialist state to rely more heavily on less dependable southern routes. Although the blockade did not produce a direct military confrontation, it placed constant pressure on Darasian commerce and naval planning.

Daras responded by adapting its trade patterns and by increasing its diplomatic efforts abroad. The blockade's economic pressure was most acute in the early years, when the new state was still adjusting to the demands of socialist reconstruction and the loss of unrestricted maritime access. Over time, Darasian officials sought a negotiated settlement that would end the blockade without requiring the state to abandon its socialist project.

== Memorandum of understanding ==

In 1911, Zong and Daras concluded a memorandum of understanding that ended the blockade. Under the agreement, Daras regained transit rights through the Jade Peninsula, while also agreeing to limit the construction of its navy and to continue paying passage fees to Zong until 1925. The settlement allowed Daras to resume maritime traffic in the Jade Sea, but it did so on terms that preserved Zong's leverage over regional shipping.

The memorandum was presented in both states as a compromise. For Zong, it confirmed that Daras would remain constrained militarily and financially. For Daras, it ended a blockade that had threatened to isolate the socialist republic from important maritime markets.

== Consequences ==

The blockade strengthened Daras' external alliances even as it restricted its shipping. Breisland, Mercia, and Volisania all regarded the embargo as evidence that Daras needed diplomatic backing against Zong, and their ties with the socialist state became more durable during the blockade years. The result was a sharper alignment between Daras and several Illypnian powers that viewed Zong as the principal maritime rival in the region.

In Darasian historiography, the blockade became an early example of how external pressure shaped the development of socialist statecraft. It reinforced the view that Daras required both industrial capacity and strategic depth in order to remain independent, and it encouraged the state to balance maritime ambition with caution in its relations with Zong.

